Body:
Can confirm that these commands can become unavailable in OpenCode `1.18.9`.

My local stdio MCP server is enabled in `opencode.json`, but the server itself isn't running. Commands such as /init do not appear.

After marking that MCP configuration as Disabled and restarting OpenCode, the /init command reappears.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "mcp": {
    "my-mcp-server": {
      "type": "remote",
      "url": "http://localhost:5858/sse",
      "enabled": true
    }
  }
}
```

@joaomj sorry, I missed your earlier question before adding this measurement.

No: my config does not explicitly set `"snapshot": false`, so this evidence does not establish behavior under that setting. Current source shows the `snapshot` option gating session Git snapshot/diff generation in `packages/opencode/src/session/summary.ts`; the database growth discussed here is retained `message.updated.1` / `message.part.updated.1` event history. I therefore would not expect that option to disable these event writes, but that is a source-based expectation rather than a controlled measurement. A useful follow-up would compare event-row and byte growth for the same output-heavy workload with snapshot enabled versus disabled.

Additional long-lived macOS instance evidence: `~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db` reached 64 GiB. A direct `dbstat` attribution query could not complete within 120 seconds under normal runtime load, so even diagnosis was operationally expensive. The sibling tool-output store separately reached 33 GiB (#29694). This strengthens the need for bounded built-in storage stats, event retention/compaction, and an idle-safe checkpoint/VACUUM workflow before the database reaches tens of GiB.
